## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

Your code uses the **timeout enforcement** pattern — `Thread.Abort()` kills a thread exceeding a deadline. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`.

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var token = cts.Token;

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(), token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException if token fires
            return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Changes

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) |
|---|---|
| `new Thread(...)` + `thread.Start()` | `Task.Run(work, token)` |
| `thread.Join(timeout)` | `task.WaitAsync(token)` |
| `thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter` (via constructor) |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed — not needed |

### Critical Pitfalls

1. **Cancellation is cooperative, not preemptive.** Unlike `Thread.Abort`, the `CancellationToken` won't interrupt `work()` mid-execution unless `work` checks the token. If `work` is a long-running CPU-bound operation that never checks, the task continues running after "timeout" — you just stop *waiting*.

2. **To make `work` truly stoppable**, pass the token into it:
   ```csharp
   public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
   {
       using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
       var token = cts.Token;
       var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);
       // ... same pattern
   }
   ```
   Then inside `work`: call `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at checkpoints.

3. **Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** — it holds timer resources. Always wrap in `using`.

4. **Uncooperative code (can't modify `work`)**: If the delegate calls third-party code that can't check tokens, the only true "kill" mechanism is to run it in a **separate process** and call `Process.Kill()`. There is no in-process equivalent to `Thread.Abort` in modern .NET.

5. **`Task.WaitAsync`** is .NET 6+ only — it detaches the wait from the task. The task itself may still run to completion in the background even after the timeout fires.

### If You Need an Async Overload

```csharp
public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
    Func<CancellationToken, Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    try
    {
        return await work(cts.Token);
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}
```

This is the preferred approach in modern .NET — fully async, fully cooperative.
